Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Contraction Paths and Execution", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"2a0fdda6-4328-42ed-b0bd-6740ceb74092"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "Once a tensor network is built, two questions remain before a number falls \
out: in what order to merge tensors, and what numerical engine to drive each \
merge. The paclet separates those concerns. A ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox[
   RowBox[{"contraction", " ", "path"}],
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/PathQ"]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"311499f4-d431-479d-a217-49297db518b8"],
 " is a pure ordering, found by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["GreedyContractionPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/GreedyContractionPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"3f5751ae-30e1-46b1-ae08-9bc0e113769e"],
 " or ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["OptimalContractionPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/OptimalContractionPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"defab1f4-634f-4f34-8ca4-b4e939c8d9e5"],
 "; an execution engine \[Dash] selected by the Method option to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["TensorNetworkContract",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TensorNetworkContract"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"c20c41ca-fe0a-4817-96f5-56c0dc3971c5"],
 " \[Dash] turns each ordered pair into a numerical operation. This tutorial \
covers the path representations, both planners and their objectives, the five \
execution back-ends, and the symbolic ",
 "Inactive",
 " form that lets you inspect a plan before evaluating it."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fd836ff1-46c8-4343-af19-e045e7bd636e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<Wolfram`TensorNetworks`\>\"", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[96]:=",ExpressionUUID->"8a830255-32dc-476a-bb87-1503d0b847bf"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["1. What a Contraction Path Is", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"480fcaa9-c1e8-4fdd-b25a-a867b5d2506b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
A contraction path is a list of integer pairs {{i,j}, \[Ellipsis]}. Each step \
removes the tensors at positions i and j from the working list and appends \
their pairwise contraction \[Dash] the opt_einsum convention, where both \
source positions disappear and the result lands at the end. After n-1 steps \
an n-tensor network collapses to a single tensor. The path is independent of \
the tensors themselves: the same path can drive the contraction of any \
network with the same hyperedge structure, which is precisely what makes a \
separate planner-then-engine pipeline worthwhile. To make this concrete, \
start with a three-tensor matrix-product-state\[Hyphen]like network: a rank-2 \
left boundary, a rank-2 middle (so it stays in two dimensions), and a rank-2 \
right boundary, sharing two bond indices. Three tensors is the smallest case \
where path choice matters \[Dash] for two tensors there's only one merge, and \
the planner is trivially correct.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e86c7ef0-24a7-417f-96af-5f45feb6a887"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"mps", "=", 
  RowBox[{"TensorNetwork", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"ArraySymbol", "[", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<A\>\"", ",", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"ArraySymbol", "[", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<B\>\"", ",", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"3", ",", "3"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"ArraySymbol", "[", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<C\>\"", ",", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"3", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"3", ",", "4"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[97]:=",ExpressionUUID->"b7b470df-254e-44a8-8892-1cbb639072b3"],

