Cell[TextData[{
 "The first ",
 "{1,2}",
 " merges positions 1 and 2 of the working list ",
 "{A, B, C}",
 " to yield ",
 "{C, AB}",
 " \[Dash] position 3 slides into position 1 (only one element was left in \
front), and the merged ",
 "AB",
 " tensor is appended at the new end. The second ",
 "{1,2}",
 " then merges ",
 "C",
 " with ",
 "AB",
 " to a single tensor. The bookkeeping is mechanical; both planners produce \
paths in exactly this form. One consequence worth internalizing early: ",
 "{1,2}",
 " in step ",
 "k",
 " refers to positions in the working list ",
 "after",
 " the ",
 "k-1",
 " prior steps have re-indexed it \[Dash] not to positions in the original \
tensor list. The opt_einsum convention keeps the bookkeeping local but means \
paths cannot be reordered or sliced without renumbering. The convenience \
function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["TreePathToPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TreePathToPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"51128aa3-a95b-46e8-8457-1e3bb103db40"],
 " performs exactly this renumbering, which is why the tree form (a section \
below) is sometimes easier to manipulate."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"08be9d72-33ca-4dd8-999a-7d5ae595db76"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["2. The Greedy Heuristic", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"1a0efa64-f5a8-431e-9349-171eb8ac5889"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["GreedyContractionPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/GreedyContractionPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"be05f35d-a2bd-44a4-9551-d211d4364e25"],
 " walks the network one pair at a time, always picking the locally cheapest \
merge. \"Cheap\" defaults to the size of the resulting tensor, optionally \
penalized by a memory term. At every step the algorithm considers each pair \
of tensors that share at least one index, scores the merge by the size of the \
resulting intermediate, and commits to the lowest-scoring pair before moving \
on. The cost is linear in the number of candidate pairs per step, so a \
network of ",
 "N",
 " tensors runs in roughly O(N^2) total \[Dash] fast enough to use as the \
default once a network grows beyond about 20 tensors, where the exponential \
dynamic-programming search of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["OptimalContractionPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/OptimalContractionPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"5b70a442-f07b-46fc-890d-1e331ebea4d6"],
 " becomes infeasible. The greedy planner forgoes any guarantee of global \
optimality, but on most tensor networks the locally cheapest choice happens \
to be close to optimal, and the gap shrinks further with the \
simulated-annealing options below. Build a deterministic five-tensor test \
network from a random graph to see the planner at work:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4ec80c1f-62de-4624-86b3-c0c7c9dcac34"],

