 CellID->188561099,ExpressionUUID->"dff0b448-b3f0-42a5-a472-5be0db2244e0"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "Four options shape the search. ",
 "\"Temperature\"",
 " perturbs the greedy choice with simulated-annealing noise (zero or ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["None",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:ref/None"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "4e524049-0d44-4782-8aa1-ab63acd1f3a4"],
 " is deterministic; positive values widen the search by sometimes accepting \
a suboptimal pair, which lets the planner escape local minima at the cost of \
reproducibility). ",
 "\"RandomSeed\"",
 " makes a noisy run reproducible by fixing the underlying random stream. ",
 "\"MaxNeighbors\"",
 " caps how many candidate pairs are scored at each step, trading quality for \
speed on very dense graphs \[Dash] useful for thousand-tensor PEPS-like \
networks where the per-step candidate list is itself large. ",
 "\"MemoryWeight\"",
 " adds a penalty proportional to peak intermediate memory, which can change \
the chosen path when the cheapest-size step would build a large transient \
tensor. A practical pattern is to run the planner three times \[Dash] with ",
 "\"Temperature\"",
 " ",
 "\[Rule]",
 " ",
 "0",
 " (default), with a small positive temperature and a fixed seed, and with ",
 "\"MemoryWeight\"",
 " ",
 "\[Rule]",
 " ",
 "2.0",
 " \[Dash] then pick whichever path has the lowest objective on the actual \
network:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9924b498-d72e-4b26-8a0e-1c943cc74ffe"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"GreedyContractionPath", "[", 
  RowBox[{"tn", ",", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<MemoryWeight\>\"", "\[Rule]", "2.0"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<Temperature\>\"", "\[Rule]", "0.5"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<RandomSeed\>\"", "\[Rule]", "1"}]}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->
  "In[102]:=",ExpressionUUID->"aaeb5879-f8c8-4278-970d-1495ebbafcf7"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"4", ",", "5"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "4"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.98806542811302*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[102]=",
 CellID->1608011568,ExpressionUUID->"eb383d01-5ef1-4831-a09f-00d1363f9bd6"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The memory penalty has rerouted the planner: instead of starting with \
positions ",
 "1",
 " and ",
 "2",
 ", it now begins at ",
 "4",
 " and ",
 "5",
 " and keeps the working tensors smaller through the run. On the small test \
network the difference in cost is modest; on a thousand-tensor lattice it can \
be the difference between contractible and out-of-memory. A reasonable rule \
of thumb: when peak memory matters more than wall-clock time \[Dash] often \
the case on a laptop or for very tall MERA stacks \[Dash] set ",
 "\"MemoryWeight\"",
 " ",
 "\[Rule]",
 " ",
 "1.0",
 " or above. When tensors are uniformly small but there are many of them, \
leave the default and accept that two adjacent tensors might briefly fuse \
into something fat. Greedy paths are also stable input for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["TensorNetworkContract",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TensorNetworkContract"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"022f49b8-b9f7-4dca-8530-a0efea5793e2"],
 ": none of the downstream execution machinery cares whether a path came from \
the greedy heuristic or the optimal planner, only that it satisfies ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["CanonicalPathQ",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/CanonicalPathQ"]],
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"3ab79a74-ffe3-4ecb-a9bd-599c41e03c0e"],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9ec6e58a-bca5-41eb-8f13-abe60a372853"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 "\"MaxNeighbors\"",
 " option caps the size of the greedy beam \[Dash] the number of candidate \
contractions considered at each step. Lower values are faster but less \
optimal."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"GreedyContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"\"\<MaxNeighbors\>\"", "\[Rule]", "3"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 "\"PreSimplify\"",
 " option (default ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["None",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:ref/None"]], "InlineFormula"],
 ") preprocesses the network \[Dash] fuses duplicate indices and removes \
trivial dimensions \[Dash] before the greedy search."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"GreedyContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"\"\<PreSimplify\>\"", "\[Rule]", "True"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Different ",
 "\"RandomSeed\"",
 " values explore different greedy paths; with non-zero ",
 "\"Temperature\"",
 " this is a stochastic local search."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"GreedyContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"\"\<Temperature\>\"", "\[Rule]", "1.0"}], ",",
   RowBox[{"\"\<RandomSeed\>\"", "\[Rule]", "7"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"GreedyContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"\"\<Temperature\>\"", "\[Rule]", "1.0"}], ",",
   RowBox[{"\"\<RandomSeed\>\"", "\[Rule]", "13"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Greedy also accepts a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["Graph",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Graph"]], "InlineFormula"],
 ", an association of ",
 "\"Dimensions\"",
 " / ",
 "\"Indices\"",
 " / ",
 "\"Contractions\"",
 " data, or an inactive ",
 "TensorContract[TensorProduct[\[Ellipsis]], \[Ellipsis]]",
 " expression \[Dash] useful when the caller already has a partial pipeline \
assembled."
}], "Text"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["3. Dynamic-Programming Optimum", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d77deac7-0594-462d-a8d2-251d62d996e9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["OptimalContractionPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/OptimalContractionPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"01ce9eec-895a-407b-822b-21b416628765"],
 " runs a dynamic-programming search over subsets of tensors and returns a \
provably optimal path for whichever objective the Method option names. \
Conceptually, the algorithm enumerates subsets of the working list and asks, \
for each subset, what its best contraction order is in terms of the subsets \
it can be split into; the answer for the full set then composes the optimal \
answer for the whole network. The search is exponential in the number of \
tensors \[Dash] roughly O(3^N) without pruning \[Dash] so it is appropriate \
for networks up to about twenty tensors, beyond which the greedy heuristic of \
the previous section is the practical choice. The default objective is ",
 "\"size\"",
 ", which minimizes the largest intermediate tensor across the entire \
contraction:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b90cdc30-5fff-41c2-991e-3b5a7520b393"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[", "tn", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->
  "In[103]:=",ExpressionUUID->"0eed9c4a-f171-4c88-a62d-caa69a59b385"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.988065428125181*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[103]=",
 CellID->1916209528,ExpressionUUID->"ca0a6fcc-2278-46ab-b13e-cc259a941ef2"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Six Method values are available, each minimizing a different scalar \
functional of the path. \"size\" (above) bounds the peak intermediate \[Dash] \
the implicit proxy for memory in the original opt_einsum library. \"flops\" \
minimizes the total floating-point operation count summed over all pairwise \
merges. \"max\" bounds the size of the largest intermediate as a tighter \
peak-memory proxy than \"size\" (which can also be sensitive to how rank-1 \
boundaries are handled). \"write\" minimizes the total bytes written across \
all intermediates, which approximates cache traffic better than raw FLOPs on \
bandwidth-bound hardware. \"combo\" is a weighted blend of size and flops, \
useful when neither objective dominates in isolation. \"limit\" minimizes the \
per-step largest tensor under a hard budget constraint and is the right \
choice when an out-of-memory error must be avoided at any cost. The size and \
flops paths differ on this network:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"3984f28b-a990-4d2c-ab9a-0ccc451b9346"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[", 
  RowBox[{"tn", ",", 
   RowBox[{"Method", "\[Rule]", "\"\<flops\>\""}]}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.988065454159293*^9, 3.988065455835713*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[120]:=",ExpressionUUID->"e6860cd5-048e-47c7-b7be-2532feb781f9"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"3", ",", "4"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.988065428142243*^9, 3.988065463968403*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[120]=",
 CellID->1956602919,ExpressionUUID->"8ab8568c-f187-4f5e-88d5-d85fc82bfaf5"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Step two flips from {1,2} (size-optimal) to {3,4} (flops-optimal): the size \
objective accepts a wider intermediate that costs more arithmetic, while the \
flops objective spreads the work across two independent left-and-right \
partial products before joining them. Which is preferable depends on the \
bottleneck \[Dash] memory or compute. For modern dense-tensor contractions on \
a workstation with abundant RAM, \"flops\" usually wins on wall-clock time; \
for memory-constrained machines or for sparse intermediates, \"size\" or \
\"limit\" is safer. For very large search spaces \"PruningThreshold\" caps \
the dynamic-programming table by discarding partial solutions whose cost \
already exceeds the threshold, trading optimality guarantees for \
tractability. With pruning, the planner stops being provably optimal but \
still returns a path \[Dash] one that is at least as good as the threshold, \
and often significantly better:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1287a1ee-bd08-4e01-ab50-db36330195e3"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[", 
  RowBox[{"tn", ",", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<PruningThreshold\>\"", "\[Rule]", "100.0"}]}], "]"}]], "Input",\

 CellLabel->
  "In[105]:=",ExpressionUUID->"3631fa2f-5a11-423d-9b58-43b49437ee5b"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9880654281548367`*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[105]=",
 CellID->2001413058,ExpressionUUID->"f802c24b-a914-4612-ba95-ce429a5a421f"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
On this five-tensor test the pruned search recovers the same path as the \
unpruned size-optimal search, because the threshold is large enough not to \
discard any winning branch. On a twenty-tensor network the pruning option is \
what makes the dynamic-programming planner usable at all; without it the \
table can hold tens of millions of entries. The cost-cap value is in the same \
units as whichever Method objective is in use, so a threshold of 100.0 \
against \"size\" means \"discard any partial solution whose largest \
intermediate already exceeds 100 elements\". Set the threshold generously at \
first, then tighten it once you see how the planner responds.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5bf070af-052d-4541-9c9e-1c312ea65fce"],

Cell["The Method option supports six cost functions:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[GridBox[{
   {Cell["Method", "TableText", FontWeight->"Bold"],
    Cell["What it minimizes", "TableText", FontWeight->"Bold"]},
   {Cell["\"size\" (default)", "TableText"],
    Cell["the size of the largest intermediate tensor", "TableText"]},
   {Cell["\"flops\"", "TableText"],
    Cell["the total floating-point operation count", "TableText"]},
   {Cell["\"max\"", "TableText"],
    Cell["the size of the single largest tensor created", "TableText"]},
   {Cell["\"write\"", "TableText"],
    Cell["the total write cost (sum of all intermediates)", "TableText"]},
   {Cell["\"combo\"", "TableText"],
    Cell["a combined size+flops cost", "TableText"]},
   {Cell["\"limit\"", "TableText"],
    Cell["a hard limit on max-intermediate-size", "TableText"]}
  }]], "DefinitionBox"],

Cell["All six values are recognized; the path that is optimal in one cost is \
generally suboptimal in another.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"Method", "\[Rule]", "\"\<size\>\""}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"Method", "\[Rule]", "\"\<max\>\""}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"Method", "\[Rule]", "\"\<write\>\""}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"Method", "\[Rule]", "\"\<combo\>\""}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"Method", "\[Rule]", "\"\<limit\>\""}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\"AllowOuterProducts\"",
 " permits the optimizer to introduce outer-product steps (pairs with no \
shared index). The default disallows them since they multiply tensor sizes \
without contracting; some networks benefit from allowing the search to \
consider them."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"\"\<AllowOuterProducts\>\"", "\[Rule]", "True"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\"PreSimplify\"",
 " mirrors the greedy option \[Dash] fuses duplicate indices and trivial \
dimensions before the dynamic-programming search runs."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[",
  RowBox[{"tn", ",",
   RowBox[{"\"\<PreSimplify\>\"", "\[Rule]", "True"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "For backwards compatibility the dispatcher ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["TensorNetworkFindContractionPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->
    "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TensorNetworkFindContractionPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"48bf1b87-f018-4275-8a9b-61ad8d562eba"],
 " still exists and forwards to one of the two routines above based on its ",
 "Method",
 " option; it issues ",
 "::deprec",
 " on each call. New code should call ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["GreedyContractionPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/GreedyContractionPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"fd1a994b-d95a-462d-b964-3e58fcc1b8ad"],
 " or ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["OptimalContractionPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/OptimalContractionPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"4aa14607-562f-4d6d-8a03-578573418814"],
 " directly. The deprecation message fires once per call:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->40238474,ExpressionUUID->"21aebc0e-c4f0-43ae-a856-6cfb28e968b9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"TensorNetworkFindContractionPath", "[", 
  RowBox[{"mps", ",", 
   RowBox[{"Method", "\[Rule]", "\"\<Optimal\>\""}]}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.988065512664908*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[121]:=",
 CellID->1978167360,ExpressionUUID->"f30e53ba-794e-40fb-ba3c-9df8c7d74b87"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TemplateBox[{
  "TensorNetworkFindContractionPath", "deprec", 
   "\"TensorNetworkFindContractionPath is deprecated. Use \
GreedyContractionPath or OptimalContractionPath instead.\"", 2, 121, 6, 
   33396362703300039060, "Local", 
   "Wolfram`TensorNetworks`TensorNetworkFindContractionPath"},
  "MessageTemplate2",
  BaseStyle->"MSG"]], "Message",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.988065513220043*^9},
 CellLabel->"During evaluation of In[121]:=",
 CellID->877243385,ExpressionUUID->"85cdf507-82df-407e-92b2-ebc3809e4713"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.988065513224749*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[121]=",
 CellID->1354335040,ExpressionUUID->"f1eed5e1-0b2b-4db1-8e82-651b529e96fe"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["4. Path Representations", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"15f12545-2102-4db1-90a6-ac51d388f26e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "Three equivalent path representations circulate inside the paclet, with \
utilities to convert between them. The flat ",
 "linear",
 " form is the opt_einsum list of pairs already seen above; positions are \
renumbered after every step so each pair refers to the working list at that \
moment. The ",
 "tree",
 " form is a nested list where each inner list pairs (or groups) two \
subtrees, recording the contraction order as a binary tree of leaves at the \
bottom and the final result at the root \[Dash] convenient for visualization, \
for engines that consume sub-products in one step, and for any operation that \
needs to look at sub-contractions as units rather than as positional pairs. \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["PathToTreePath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/PathToTreePath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"fdf8ed80-4bf3-4880-9f95-837855ec289a"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["TreePathToPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TreePathToPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"21df6ed8-38a5-4e76-ab87-ae48c056fe2d"],
 " convert in either direction; they are exact inverses up to the choice of \
how leaves are labeled (numeric tensor positions by default)."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"44d28902-9d11-47af-a578-50b4f07f672e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"tree", "=", 
  RowBox[{"PathToTreePath", "[", 
   RowBox[{"OptimalContractionPath", "[", 
    RowBox[{"tn", ",", 
     RowBox[{"Method", "\[Rule]", "\"\<flops\>\""}]}], "]"}], 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->
  "In[122]:=",ExpressionUUID->"621ccbc1-0b6f-439f-8475-b214bc0efc67"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", "3", "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", "4", "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", "5", "}"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], 
  "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.988065428200055*^9, 3.988065518813444*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[122]=",
 CellID->556677612,ExpressionUUID->"28b4454e-0126-4868-a635-a036dceb726c"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Read the tree from the leaves outward: ",
 "{1}",
 " and ",
 "{2}",
 " pair first (the linear ",
 "{1,2}",
 " step), giving an inner pair ",
 "{{1},{2}}",
 ". Then ",
 "{5}",
 " joins that group, then ",
 "{4}",
 " joins the resulting subtree, and ",
 "{3}",
 " joins last as the outermost pair. The shape is left-leaning because the \
flops path repeatedly merges the most recently formed intermediate with the \
next dangling leaf, rather than building two balanced halves; the planner \
found that this scheduling minimizes total FLOPs given the bond dimensions of \
this network. ",
 "TreePathToPath",
 " inverts the conversion in the obvious way, recursively flattening the tree \
into the linear positional form. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["CanonicalPath",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/CanonicalPath"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"4a75c9d8-f6d7-44c8-a029-7c56a35efa24"],
 " normalizes a path that may include unary identity steps ",
 "{i}",
 " (which arise when an earlier ",
 "BinaryTensorNetwork",
 " pass binarizes a single hyperedge) by pushing them into pair form. A path \
is canonical when every step is a pair; both planners return canonical \
paths."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7e7a0b84-23d7-4584-8270-ba48ceac3196"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["PathIndexContractions",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/PathIndexContractions"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"2bf8a5d2-6880-4ecb-98d4-c75c81c7b688"],
 " shows, for each step, which indices were summed out. Pass the path and the \
hyperedge list. When the network has hyper-edges (any index shared by three \
or more tensors), use the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["BinaryTensorNetwork",
   BaseStyle->"Link",
   ButtonData->"paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/BinaryTensorNetwork"]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"ddcd11d9-3494-420f-befb-7b4359b61fd6"],
 " hyperedges so the index labels match what the planner sees:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"768ed181-cab0-4b7f-93f9-070d54462996"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"PathIndexContractions", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"GreedyContractionPath", "[", "tn", "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"BinaryTensorNetwork", "[", "tn", "]"}], "[", "\"\<Hyperedges\>\"",
     "]"}]}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->
  "In[123]:=",ExpressionUUID->"b704d7a7-6634-462d-bb38-2be064c193ed"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", "4", "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"13", ",", "15"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"12", ",", "14"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"7", ",", "11"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9880654282156763`*^9, 3.9880655219257603`*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[123]=",
